= Genetic inheritance (simple) =

'''Genetic inheritance''' is how living things pass genetic material to new cells or offspring. Genes are parts of DNA, which contains instructions used by cells.

When cells divide, they copy their DNA and pass copies to daughter cells. In many animals and plants, offspring receive genetic material from two parents. Changes in DNA and the reshuffling of chromosomes help create differences between individuals.

Bacteria also pass DNA when they divide, and they can sometimes pick up genes from other bacteria. Some biological properties are influenced by inherited regulatory marks as well as DNA sequence, but not every temporary change is inherited across generations.

Inheriting DNA does not mean being an identical copy of a parent.

